The '''McLaren W1''' is a limited-production [[Hybrid electric vehicle|hybrid]] [[sports car]] developed by [[McLaren Automotive]]. It is the successor to the [[McLaren P1]]. It was unveiled on October 6, 2024.<ref>{{cite web |author=Laurent Sanson |date=27 September 2024 |title=McLaren W1 2025: The ultimate supercar approaching |url=https://www.auto-mag.fr/actualites/mclaren-w1-2025-la-supercar-ultime-en-approche/ |access-date=2024-11-22 |website=Auto-Mag |language=fr}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |author=Brad Anderson |date=27 September 2024 |title=McLaren Names Its Next Hypercar W1 Ahead Of Oct 6 Reveal |url=https://www.carscoops.com/2024/09/mclaren-teases-p1-successor-ahead-of-october-6-unveiling/ |access-date=2024-11-22 |website=Carscoops}}</ref> Production was scheduled to commence in 2025, and was limited to 399 units.{{Update inline|date=May 2026}}

==Specifications==
McLaren reports<ref>{{Cite news |title=McLaren introduces US$2.1 million W1 hybrid supercar to honour F1 history |date=7 October 2024 |url=https://www.businesstimes.com.sg/lifestyle/mclaren-introduces-us2-1-million-w1-hybrid-supercar-honour-f1-history |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241121012443/https://www.businesstimes.com.sg/lifestyle/mclaren-introduces-us2-1-million-w1-hybrid-supercar-honour-f1-history |archive-date=2024-11-21 |access-date=2024-12-15 |work=The Business Times |language=en}}</ref> the W1 as having a dry weight of {{cvt|1,399|kg}} and a maximum power output of {{cvt|1275|PS|kW hp|lk=in}}, resulting in a power-to-weight ratio of 911 PS/[[tonne]]. It features a [[hybrid powertrain]], combining a 4.0-liter [[twin-turbo]] flat-plane crank [[V8 engine]] (designated MHP-8) with an electric module that includes a [[Radial flux motor|radial flux]] electric motor and a [[motor controller|Motor Control Unit (MCU)]]. The electric assistance is designed to improve [[throttle response]] and performance, with a total system output of {{cvt|1275|PS|kW}}<ref>{{Cite web |author=Wesley Wren |date=7 October 2024 |title=McLaren W1 Is a 1,258-HP Hybrid Hypercar |url=https://www.autoweek.com/news/a62521382/mclaren-w1-reveal-specs-features/ |access-date=2024-10-17 |website=[[Autoweek]]}}</ref> and {{cvt|1340|Nm}} of torque.<ref name="outrageous">{{cite news |author=Shafiq Abidin |date=8 October 2024 |title=Nine of the most outrageous numbers developed by the McLaren W1 |url=https://www.topgear.com/car-news/first-look/nine-most-outrageous-numbers-developed-mclaren-w1 |access-date=2024-11-22 |website=Top Gear}}</ref> 

The W1 has an estimated top speed of 350 km/h (220 mph), capable of accelerating from 0-100 km/h (0-62 mph) in 2.7 seconds. From a standstill, the vehicle is able to reach 200 km/h (124 mph) in approximately 5.8 seconds.<ref name="firstlook">{{cite news |author=Andrew Beckford |date=6 October 2024 |title=McLaren W1 First Look: Say Hello to McLaren's New Halo Hypercar |url=https://www.motortrend.com/news/2026-mclaren-w1-first-look-review/ |access-date=2024-11-22 |website=Motortrend}}</ref>

Internal components feature a 62-liter fuel tank and onboard software intended for fuel-efficiency optimization.

== Design ==
The floor and footwell of the W1 are positioned higher than those of the P1, modifying airflow beneath the car. The active aerodynamics system has been updated, with the rear wing controlled by electric motors. The wing can raise, lower, and rotate, functioning as both a [[downforce]] aid and an [[Air brake (aeronautics)|air brake]] while also incorporating a [[drag reduction system|DRS]] mode.<ref>{{Cite web |title=This is the McLaren W1, McLaren's bonkers successor to the F1 and P1 hyper cars. |author=Avon Middleton |date=6 October 2024 |url=https://www.topgear.co.za/news/this-is-the-mclaren-w1-mclarens-bonkers-successor-to-the-f1-and-p1-hyper-cars |access-date=2024-12-07 |website=topgear.co.za}}</ref>

The W1's cooling system is structured to manage [[Thermal shock|thermal loads]], particularly in high-performance driving conditions. It incorporates three separate water-glycol circuits, each operating at different temperatures:
* A high-temperature circuit with four [[Radiator|radiators]] for engine cooling.
* A low-temperature circuit for charge cooling.
* A dedicated hybrid cooling circuit for the [[Automatic transmission|E-module]], battery, charger, and [[DC-to-DC converter|DC/DC converter]].

A total of 10 [[heat exchanger]]s<ref name="firstlook" /> are included to manage general cooling needs, including [[HVAC]] and oil cooling.

The W1 features an eight-speed [[Dual-clutch transmission|dual-clutch transmission (DCT)]]<ref name="firstlook" /> designed to handle high [[torque]] levels. A [[Hydraulic machinery|hydraulic]] E-differential determines the torque split between the rear wheels, [[Traction control system|enhancing mid-corner traction]].

The W1's E-module contributes up to {{cvt|347|PS|kW|disp=flip}} and is mounted beside the transmission. It features:
* A {{cvt|1.384|kWh|disp=flip}} battery designed for rapid power delivery.
* Active aero stemming from multiple downforce controls using the spoiler and front bumper.
* Second-generation [[Dielectric|dielectric immersion]] cooling for effective thermal management.
* Onboard charging capability, allowing the battery to be replenished to 80% in 22&nbsp;minutes.
* McLaren’s Active Long Tail Rear Wing, providing multiple modes of downforce.

The W1 incorporates [[Ground effect (aerodynamics)|ground-effect]] [[aerodynamics]], including an underbody that enhances airflow and downforce, drawing influence from the [[McLaren|McLaren Formula 1 Team's]] engineering, and active aerodynamic elements, including an adjustable front [[Splitter plate (aeronautics)|splitter]] and rear wing.

Internal [[wind tunnel]] tests verify that the W1 has a maximum total downforce of {{cvt|1000|kgf|kN}}.<ref name="outrageous" />

